# Two phases: develop, then release on the owner's word

The factory used to run one undivided pass — research, implement, build the signed APK and
AAB, compose every store asset, capture screenshots, verify — with no point at which the
owner could say "this app is right". The owner therefore inspected the UI *after* the
expensive, irreversible half was paid for, and a UI he rejected meant the whole release cost
had been spent on a revision nobody wanted.

The pipeline is now two phases with an explicit human gate between them. **Building, signing,
screenshotting and store-pack assembly are release work and do not happen during
development.**

## Phase 1 — development (`/forge` or `/ship`)

`/forge` is the whole pipeline from one command — its step A is the `research` and `ideation` row
below — and `/ship` enters at the same place when the idea is already in hand. Both stop at the same
two checkpoints; neither may skip one because the other ran the stage before it.

The phase ends when there is an app the owner can look at:

- research, ideation, `economics.json`, and **human checkpoint 1** (approve the plan),
- the slot, the Kotlin/Compose implementation, the tests,
- unit tests through `node scripts/run-slot-tests.mjs --slot <slug>`,
- a debug build the agent installs on the connected phone and drives itself
  (`node scripts/verify.mjs --slot <slug> --build` compiles `assembleDebug`; the APK is
  `app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk`),
- source-level gates: `node scripts/verify.mjs --slot <slug>`.
- **human checkpoint 1.5**: the owner decides the **product** — whether this is the app to ship —
  and approves the release in his own words. **Testing on the device is the agent's own work, done
  before this checkpoint**: the agent runs the app, and the handoff carries the observed evidence —
  the device serial, the hash of the installed artifact and what was tapped and what appeared —
  because "it compiles" and "the tests are green" are not a substitute for it
  (`.cursor/rules/device-testing.mdc`). This is not where the owner verifies the code, and a handoff
  that asks him to install, launch or test the app is a failed checkpoint, not a satisfied one.

Allowed in phase 1: `--init` drafts of `store/listing.json`, `verify.mjs`, unit tests,
a **debug** build. Cheap and reversible.

Forbidden in phase 1 — these are release actions and are now gated in code:

- `node scripts/prepare-release.mjs` (release APK + AAB + signing + release manifest),
- `python scripts/store/render.py` in its rendering modes (icon, feature graphic, screenshot
  sets), and `python scripts/store-studio.py` in any mode — the composed identity, which also
  rewrites the app's own launcher vectors,
- `node scripts/media-capture.mjs` (device screenshots),
- `node scripts/sign-release.mjs --generate` — generating a signing key, which mints the app's
  permanent identity together with the password that unlocks it (`authorization.mdc` governs the
  separate question of whether a key may be created at all).

Phase 1 ends by **stopping and asking**. The report says the app is ready to review, names
what to look at, and waits. It does not prepare the release "while I am here".

## The gate — the owner's approval

The owner reviews the app and says so in his own words. That decision is recorded, once:

```bash
node scripts/approve-release.mjs --slot <slug> --note "<the owner's own words>"
node scripts/approve-release.mjs --check  --slot <slug>   # what the release scripts call
node scripts/approve-release.mjs --revoke --slot <slug>   # always safe
```

Rules for the recording step:

- **Run it only when the owner has explicitly approved the release in the current
  conversation.** The agent may not decide the app is good enough and approve it on his
  behalf. This is the same discipline as `--approve-declarations` in `play-upload.mjs`.
- **`--note` is required and must be the owner's own words.** A record with no words is a
  boolean an agent could set by itself; the note is what makes it a decision a later reader
  can audit. Never paraphrase a vague "ok" into an approval — ask what he approved.
- Approval authorises **the release pipeline** (signed build, store pack, captures), and it is what
  lets the agent tick the two first-publication legal declarations during console submission: the
  approval is the product checkpoint, and the click is not. **Console submission and Publish are the
  agent's job** after a separate publish yes (checkpoint 2 below) — the owner does not perform the
  console labour (`authorization.mdc`). The owner's decision, recorded 2026-10-03: «В нашем заводе
  есть правило, что перед публикацией необходимо одобрение владельца. Собственно говоря, в то время
  и я проверяю проверку всех вот этих политик, и только после моего одобрения я говорю разрешить
  публикацию. Поэтому, если я разрешил публикацию, значит эти галочки можно уже автоматически
  нажимать. Потому что все проверки необходимые для публикации проводятся до начальной публикации
  всего приложения.»

The record is `apps/<slot>/release-approval.json`, gitignored, and the check is one module:
`scripts/lib/release-approval.mjs`. Six entry points call it and refuse without it:
`prepare-release.mjs`, `scripts/store/render.py`, `scripts/store-studio.py`, `media-capture.mjs`,
`play-upload.mjs` and `scripts/sign-release.mjs --generate`. The last two were added on 2026-10-01,
after an audit found this paragraph claiming more than the code delivered: the studio composed the
whole store identity and rewrote the launcher vectors with no gate at all, and the signer minted a
key with no gate — the violation `authorization.mdc` was written from.

**What the gate still cannot stop, stated rather than implied.** A raw `./gradlew assembleRelease`
signs once a key exists, and a direct in-process `import` of the leaf writers in `store/render.py`
skips its check. Neither is a CLI entry point; catching them needs a sensor that reads a slot for
release work with no approval behind it, and no such sensor exists yet. The gate is six doors, not
a sealed room.

## The record is bound to the revision, not the slot name

The approval stores a digest of every build input in the slot (plus the shared
`capabilities/` tree). **Any edit to the app voids it**, because the approved revision no
longer exists; the refusal names the command that re-records it. Restoring the approved
bytes re-opens the gate, because the content is identical to what the owner reviewed.

`store/`, `dist/`, `captures/`, `build/` and machine-local state are deliberately *excluded*
from the digest: the release pipeline writes them after approval, so including them would
make the pipeline void its own approval mid-run. `capabilities.json`, `economics.json`,
`.kiln-state.json` and `README.md` are excluded too — they describe the slot but do not
change the built bytes. The two record files — `release-approval.json` and
`device-verification.json` — are excluded for a third reason: each is written and then
checked, and a record that stores the revision's digest must not be an input to the digest
it stores. The list, with the reason each name is exempt for, is `EXCLUDED_FILE_REASONS` in
`scripts/lib/release-approval.mjs`.

If the digest ever disagrees with a change the owner actually wanted, the fix is to re-record
the approval, never to weaken the check.

## Phase 2 — release (`/release`)

Runs only after the gate passes:

1. `node scripts/prepare-release.mjs --slot <slug>` — one Gradle invocation: debug + release
   APK + AAB, signature verified, artifacts staged, `dist/release-manifest.json` written.
2. `python scripts/store/render.py --slot <slug>` — listing copy, icon, feature graphic and the
   full screenshot inventory.
3. `node scripts/media-capture.mjs --slot <slug>` when a device is attached; real captures
   beat synthetic ones, and any synthetic capture must be labelled as such.
4. `node scripts/verify.mjs --slot <slug> --build`, then `/eval` and
   `play-auditor`.
5. Update `registry/apps.json`, then **human checkpoint 2 — publish authorization**: the agent asks
   whether it may proceed to publish; on the owner's yes, the agent walks
   `docs/play-publishing-playbook.md` §5.1 end to end, including forms, declarations, the release
   form, submit, and Publish. Checkpoint 1.5 authorised the release pipeline; checkpoint 2
   authorises the console submission. The owner is yes/no and corrections — not the person who
   fills the console while the agent watches. The owner's decision, recorded 2026-10-04: after the
   app is developed and confirmed ready, the agent must not propose that the owner publish while
   the agent watches; the agent asks «may I proceed to publish?», and on yes the agent itself opens
   Google Play Console, fills every form, uploads what tooling allows, fixes what needs fixing, and
   publishes — the owner helps only where a tooling limit requires a human (store-graphic file
   inputs, AAB file chooser, promo-code files).

Report the release phase's own elapsed wall clock for transparency; do not present development time
as if it covered the release. Elapsed time is never an idea-selection or kill criterion.

## Tooling assists — the three points where the owner's hand is needed

A few console acts cannot be automated and must not be faked: hidden `input[type=file]` controls
(store graphics appear only after `Добавить объекты`), a bundle chooser the sandboxed browser
denies, and **a file download** the agent can trigger but cannot receive — the browser answers it
with a native save window that is outside the page. That is an unsolved tooling limitation, recorded
as a limitation and not as a policy (`docs/play-console-checklist.md` §1.B and §10) — it is why the
agent asks for a human hand at those points, **not** why the owner owns publication.

There are **exactly three**, and they are not the same kind of act. Two push a file *into* the
console; the third pulls a file *out*.

| # | Point | Where | What the agent does | What the owner does |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | **AAB / APK chooser** | the release form's bundle upload | opens the page, names the absolute path under `dist/` | drops the file |
| 2 | **Store-graphic inputs** | `…/store-listings/default/edit`, the five image fields | opens the page, names the paths under `store/assets/` | drops the files |
| 3 | **Promo-code download** | `Монетизация с помощью Play → Promotions` (`.../promotions`) | clicks **`Скачать коды` once** | confirms the browser's save window |

**For 1 and 2 — uploads.** The owner's instruction, recorded 2026-10-03 and reframed 2026-10-04, is
that the agent **drives the session, opens the page, names the paths, asks for the assist, waits,
then continues and publishes**:

1. **Open the page** in the browser the factory drives, scrolled to the field in question
   (`…/store-listings/default/edit` at `Значок приложения`, or the release upload page for the
   bundle chooser).
2. **Give the exact instruction**: which file goes into which field, as absolute paths — the icon
   from `store/assets/icon-512.png`, the feature graphic from
   `store/assets/feature-graphic-1024x500.png`, the phone set from `store/assets/<locale>/phone-*.png`
   and the tablet sets from `store/assets/<locale>/tablet-*.png` (or the staged AAB under `dist/`).
3. **Ask, and wait.** The owner drops files into the open chooser / fields and confirms; the agent
   does not proceed, does not re-save over that upload, and does not leave the release half-done.
   **While files are being chosen, the agent touches the page as little as possible** — it must not
   navigate away or reload it, because the chosen files live in the form and not on the server until
   the next save, so a reload silently discards the work and leaves the agent checking an empty form
   it reloaded itself.
4. **Only then continue** — save / `Далее` / the rest of §5.1, including Publish — reading the
   validation messages the console produces. A reload is not a check.

**For 3 — the promo-code download.** This is the point the list was already naming and the procedure
was not, and the defect is recorded: the agent pressed `Скачать коды` repeatedly, and each press
opened another save window the owner had to dismiss. The protocol is one click and a wait, never a
retry loop:

1. **Navigate** to `Монетизация с помощью Play → Promotions` (`.../promotions`) and click
   **`Скачать коды` exactly once**.
2. **Stop and ask** — in the owner's own language, e.g. «Нажмите „Скачать“ в открывшемся окне и
   подтвердите, когда файл сохранится» — then **wait**. The agent does not click again, does not
   browse away from the page, and does not reload it while the window is open.
3. **On the owner's confirmation**, continue: read the downloaded CSV and use it for what §5 of the
   checklist needs (the reviewer credentials' `Другие данные, которые требуются для входа`). **The
   file is never committed** — a code is a free unlock of what the slot sells.
4. **If no window opened**, say so, and hand the owner the manual path so he can do it himself:
   the page is `.../promotions`, the control is `Скачать коды`, Play names the file
   **`promotion_codes.csv`**, and a browser saves it to its default download folder (on Windows
   usually `%USERPROFILE%\Downloads`). When he tells the agent where it landed, the agent reads that
   path and finishes the step.

**Never press `Скачать коды` a second time.** A second press opens a second save window and leaves
the owner dismissing duplicates; the button is idempotent from the agent's side and the waiting is
not optional. `docs/play-console-checklist.md` §5 and §10 and `docs/play-publishing-playbook.md`
carry the same three points and the same one-click rule — `harness.publishAssists` compares all the
holders, so a fourth document cannot drift or a point cannot go missing quietly.

The assist ask is mandatory because those acts are invisible to every gate: the pack validates,
`media-capture.mjs` has already photographed the build, and skipping the ask reports a finished
release whose store page has no icon (or no bundle, or a reviewer with no way into paid content).

## What never changes

- A green gate is not a reason to skip the owner's review. The gate decides *when* release
  work may run, not whether the app is good.
- Never record an approval to unblock yourself. If the release scripts refuse, the answer is
  to ask the owner, not to delete or edit `release-approval.json`.
- Never upload to Play or press Publish without an explicit publish yes in the current conversation
  (`authorization.mdc`). On that yes, the agent drives the console and publishes; it does not hand
  the labour back to the owner. The two first-publication declarations are ticked by the agent under
  the recorded release approval (checkpoint 1.5).
